+## Using miniccpy
+
+Requires `miniccpy` on the Python path (`pip install` it, or point
+`--miniccpy-path /path/to/miniccpy` at a source checkout).
+
+```bash
+python extract_hamiltonian.py H2O-DUCC.out H2O-DUCC.6-31g.ducc.results.txt --cc-method ccsd
+python extract_hamiltonian.py H2O-DUCC.out H2O-DUCC.6-31g.ducc.results.txt --cc-method ccsdt --cc-convergence 1e-8
@@ -113 +138,7 @@ hamiltonian_extractor/
-### Key Components
+`--cc-method` accepts any method miniccpy implements (`mp2`, `ccsd`,
+`ccsdt`, `ccsdtq`, `cc3`, `lrccsd`, ...). The extracted integrals are
+converted to miniccpy's spin-orbital, antisymmetrized-integral convention by
+`HamiltonianData.spin_orbital_integrals()`. If `h5py` or `pyscf.symm` (two of
+miniccpy's own dependencies) aren't available, pure-NumPy shims are used
+automatically so miniccpy's in-memory DIIS and amplitude printing still work
+without them.
